/** siman 069 translations — part 1: mechaber, beer-hagolah, mateh-yehonatan, pitchei-teshuva, rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd, kaf-hachayim */
export const TRANSLATIONS_PART1 = {
  'mechaber': {
    '1#main': `Laws of melicha and hadacha. And it contains 21 seifim. One must rinse the meat before melicha (and if the butcher rinsed it, one need not rinse it in the house) (Tur). And if after he rinsed he cut each piece into two or three (or removed the hooves of the legs after the rinsing) (HaAroch general rule 4 and 17) he must return and rinse them. {Rama: And if he did not do so it is as if not rinsed at all (there). The rinsing of meat l'chatchila he should be careful to soak it for up to half an hour and to rinse it well in the soak-water; but if he did not soak it, only rinsed it well, it suffices for him; and afterward he should wait a little until the water drips off before salting so the salt not dissolve from the water and not expel blood (hagahot of Shach Devarim and hagahot of Ashiri part 25 and in HaAroch general rule 1). And they are accustomed not to use other things in the vessel in which they soak meat; and if the meat was delayed in its soaking from time to time, the meat and also the vessel are forbidden — and see below in this siman; but less than from time to time one need not be stringent; and even where the vessel became forbidden, it is permitted to return and soak in it (HaAroch) — law of meat full of ice — how they conduct with its soaking — see above siman 68.}`,
    '10#main': `An idolater serving in an Israelite's house placed meat in a pot and it is not known whether he rinsed it — if the idolater knows Jewish practice, we rely on his words if there was an Israelite going in and out or any minor of understanding. {Rama: And one of these suffices (Tur). Or through masiach lefi tumo that he rinsed it well, or an Israelite even a minor going in and out, for he is afraid since he knows Jewish customs; however if he warned the idolater not to rinse without permission and he transgressed his words, forbidden, for we see he is not afraid and one may not rely on his words either (hagahot of Shach Devarim and Orchot Hayim).}`,
    '11#main': `Meat that was cooked without melicha requires sixty in the dish against that meat, and then all is permitted. {Rama: And some forbid that piece even where there are sixty against the piece (Tur in the name of one authority and hagahot of Maimoniyot in the name of Semak and hagahot of Shach Devarim and HaAroch and Maharai and Mordechai in the name of Raavan) and so is the custom if not for need such as honoring Shabbat or honoring guests, for then one may rely on the lenient authorities (Beit Yosef in the name of Orchot Chaim and another opinion). And if a piece was salted and not salted properly, its law is as if not salted at all; and likewise meat that remained three days without melicha — even if salted, its law is as if not salted; and if cooked, requires sixty against it (Orchot Hayim).}`,
    '12#main': `Meat that remained three days from time to time without melicha — its blood dried inside and will no longer come out through melicha, and one may not eat it cooked, only roasted; and after roasting it one shall not cook it; and if they cooked it, permitted (and one should not leave meat three days without melicha, for we are concerned lest they cook it (Beit Yosef in the name of Piskei Maharai siman 191).}`,
    '13#main': `And if they soaked it in water within the three days, he may leave it another three days less half an hour (Agur) (meat that was salted and a doubt whether salted within three — permitted) (HaAroch).`,
    '14#main': `Meat that remained three days without melicha and that piece became mixed with other pieces — nullified in the majority and permitted to cook all of them, even if it was fit to honor. (And the same law if cooked without melicha and afterward became mixed with others (Orchot Hayim).)`,
    '15#main': `Meat soiled with blood that was soaked in water from time to time — some forbid eating it except roasted unless there are sixty in the water against it (see s.k. 56. And some forbid even for roasting, and so is the custom) (Orchot Hayim).`,
    '16#main': `One may salt only in a perforated vessel or on straw and stubble or in a sloped place such that if water is poured there it exits immediately. {Rama: And a smooth board — water flows from it — one need not place it on a slope; but if it is not smooth one must place it on a slope so the water exits (Shaarei Dura and in Beit Yosef in the name of Teshuvot HaRosh siman 172). And even in a perforated vessel one should be careful l'chatchila that the holes be open (HaAroch general rule 3). Therefore they do not stand it on the ground, for it is like a non-perforated vessel (see below siman 70). And likewise they are somewhat stringent to place straw or stubble inside the perforated vessel, for the meat will block the holes (hagahot of Ashiri part 25 and HaAroch general rule 1). And b'dieved one need not be concerned for all this. (Another opinion). }And if he salted in a non-perforated vessel, forbidden to use that vessel for something boiling. {Rama: And if he used it, requires peeling if it is a dry matter (Rashba). And if it is a wet matter we require sixty against the peel from the bowl (Beit Yosef in the name of one authority). }And some say that even cold it is forbidden to use without rinsing; and if he used without rinsing, he rinses what he used. {Rama: But permitted to return and salt meat in it after perforating it, or even without perforating if the meat already remained in its melicha and was rinsed (Beit Yosef in the name of hagahot of Shach Devarim and HaAroch).}`,
    '17#main': `But if it is perforated, permitted to eat in it even boiling; and some forbid in boiling (and one should be careful l'chatchila and b'dieved permitted) (HaAroch general rule 3).`,
    '18#main': `Meat salted in a non-perforated vessel and remained there for the time that they would put water on the fire and it would begin to boil — all that is in the tzir is forbidden to eat even roasted; and the part of the piece outside the tzir is forbidden from it only as much as a peel, even if there is fat in it. {Rama: And some forbid the whole piece even what is outside the tzir (Tur and Mordechai in the name of Maharif). And even if he salted only a little as is the way they salt for roasting, and even if he did not remain for the measure of melicha only a little until tzir is seen in the vessel (Mordechai end of chapter 25 in the name of Rabeiah and hagahot of Shach Devarim and HaAroch general rule 10). And so is the custom and one should not change; nevertheless it is forbidden only that piece placed below in the vessel touching the tzir; but other pieces salted above it with it are permitted (there in HaAroch and in Kol Bo) and so is the custom.}`,
    '19#main': `After the meat was salted and rinsed, permitted to place it even in water that is not boiling; and there is one who requires placing it in boiling water; and the custom follows the first view, and so is the essential law (Beit Yosef and his own opinion).`,
    '2#main': `If he salted and did not rinse first, he shall rinse it and salt it a second time; and some forbid. {Rama: And so they are accustomed even if he only salted a little as is the way they salt for roasting, and even if he did not remain in his salt the measure of melicha (HaAroch and Shach Devarim and Semag); however in a place of great loss there is to permit (Beit Yosef). And if he did not rinse only a little before he salted it, permitted b'dieved; and the same law if there were sixty in the piece against the blood on it (hagahot of Shach Devarim in the name of Maharai). And if a piece was salted without rinsing with other pieces, the other pieces are permitted and it is forbidden (HaAroch general rule 4 law 8).}`,
    '20#main': `Meat that was salted and remained for the measure of melicha and afterward was placed in a vessel without rinsing and became full of tzir — permitted; and according to this, meat that fell into tzir emerging from the meat after it remained for the measure of melicha is permitted; and there is one who forbids in this and in that, and one should be concerned l'chatchila. {Rama: And some forbid (Tosafot and Rosh and several later authorities) even b'dieved as much as a peel (hagahot of Shach Devarim and in Shaarei Dura and Mahariv and Maharash and Beit Yosef and HaAroch). And so is the custom; and specifically what is placed inside the tzir; but what is outside the tzir is permitted; and the vessel into which that tzir fell is forbidden; and likewise if the vessel was a non-Jewish vessel and has residue, the meat is forbidden, for even after the measure of melicha it is considered boiling (hagahot of Shach Devarim and Teshuvot HaRosh siman 159). But if it is great loss and there is also need of a festive meal, there is to be lenient and say that after it remained the measure of melicha its tzir is not considered boiling (another opinion, and see below siman 91). }And according to his words, even though the meat remained in salt for the measure, forbidden to eat from it until he rinses it very well; and forbidden to cut from it with a knife before rinsing; and if he cut, he must kasher it. {Rama: And some permit cutting with a knife after it remained the measure of melicha, for melicha does not apply to vessels, and so is the essential law that it is permitted (Teshuvot HaRosh siman 152 and Orchot Hayim general rule 37). But the knife requires rinsing afterward or sticking in the ground if the tzir dried on it (HaAroch). And even before the measure of melicha the prohibition is not because of the knife but because visible blood came on the meat; and if he wishes to rinse there well and return and salt there, permitted. }And when they wish to make preserved meat to keep after it remained in its salt in a perforated vessel for the proper measure, they rinse it very well and afterward return and salt it so it will keep; and even in a non-perforated vessel; and even according to this view, if he wishes to salt and eat roasted without rinsing he may do so and need not be concerned for blood on the salt, for fire draws it and prevents the salt from absorbing blood — and these matters are when he salts and raises it to roast; but if it remained in its salt the salt absorbs blood and is forbidden; therefore he rinses it very well and roasts and eats (and see below siman 76 regarding meat that was salted and not rinsed and was roasted thus).}`,
    '21#main': `In a place where salt is not available, they roast the meat until all its blood drips out and afterward cook it.`,
    '3#main': `One may not salt with fine salt like flour nor with very coarse salt that falls off the meat from one side to the other (and if there is no other salt only fine salt like flour, permitted to salt with it) (another opinion).`,
    '4#main': `He shall spread salt on it so no place remain without salt, and salt so it not be fit to eat with that salt, and he need not add more salt than this; and salt it from two sides, and birds must be salted also from inside; and if he salted them only from inside or from outside, and likewise a piece that was salted only on one side — permitted. {Rama: And some forbid even b'dieved (Maharai in hagahot of Shach Devarim and HaAroch general rule 6) and so is the custom if not for need; and specifically if already cooked thus; but if not yet cooked he shall not cook it thus unless within twelve hours of salting he returns and salts the side not yet salted and cooks it afterward; and if after twelve hours then he shall roast it, for fire draws and the side already salted does not absorb from the side not salted (there).}`,
    '5#main': `After the piece was salted, if he cut from it he need not return and salt the place of the cut.`,
    '6#main': `The measure of staying in salt is not less than the span of a mil, which is approximately a third of an hour. {Rama: And on this one may rely b'dieved or even l'chatchila for honoring guests or Shabbat need; but otherwise the custom is to remain in melicha the measure of an hour and one should not change (Maharai in Teshuvot HaRosh siman 167 and hagahot of Shach Devarim and Orchot Hayim general rule 1).}`,
    '7#main': `Before putting the meat in the vessel in which they rinse it, he shall shake off the salt on it or rinse it in water; and afterward put the meat in the vessel in which they rinse and rinse it twice and wash the vessel between rinsing and rinsing. {Rama: And some say they need to rinse the meat three times (Chiddushei Agudah in the name of Or Zarua and Maharai and Orchot Hayim chapter 21 law 15 and hagahot) and so they are accustomed l'chatchila; therefore he shall rinse it or shake off the salt from it and rinse it twice, for this is as rinsing three times; or he puts the water in the vessel and afterward places the meat in it and rinses it three times, and so is the custom; and l'chatchila he puts much water in the first rinsing so the power of the salt in the tzir be nullified (another opinion). Permitted to rinse the meat in fruit juice and water is not required (Ran chapter of the slaughterer and Tosafot there).}`,
    '8#main': `If he did not shake off the salt on it and did not rinse it, there is no prohibition because the water in the vessel nullifies the power of the salt. {Rama: And even if the water is scant, nevertheless they nullify the power of the tzir (another opinion and HaAroch general rule 6 and Maharai). And some permit even if there was no water at all in the vessel and he placed the meat in it, for one need not be concerned in such a short time while busy rinsing the meat (Shach Devarim and Orchot Hayim general rule 1 and 10 and see above siman 60). And one may rely on them, and in all this one need not be concerned only in the first rinsing; but after the first rinsing one need not be concerned (as appears from Mordechai and Ran). And there is no difference whether they rinsed in a kosher vessel or treifah or a non-Jewish vessel; and if there was a little water in the vessel that nullifies the power of the salt so it is no longer considered boiling, even if the vessel is a non-Jewish vessel or treifah, still in prohibition permitted (HaAroch).}`,
    '9#main': `Meat that was salted and cooked without a final rinsing requires sixty against the salt on it. {Rama: And the whole pot combines for sixty (Shach Devarim and see s.k. 34). And if in the pot there is as much as the piece that was salted and not rinsed, all is permitted, for certainly there are sixty against the salt on the piece, for the piece itself is certainly thirty against the salt on it (HaAroch general rule 11 law 4). And if there are not sixty in the pot against the salt, even if placed only in a kli sheni, all is forbidden, for since there is salt and tzir there, even in a kli sheni it cooks (there law 1 and in Mordechai). And dry meat may be permitted even in a kli rishon, for certainly there is sixty against the salt on it since it already dried (Mordechai chapter 25 and Ramban and Beit Yosef in the name of hagahot of Ashiri and Shach Devarim). But l'chatchila he should be careful even with dry meat not to cook it or rinse it in water where yad soledes bo applies without a final rinsing (Shach Devarim there). And all this deals when he did not rinse finally at all; but if he rinsed only once and cooked thus, permitted, for b'dieved it suffices with one rinsing finally (Maharai in hagahot of Shach Devarim). Salt that meat was salted in once is forbidden to salt in a second time (Kol Bo and another opinion and the opinion of most poskim). And all the more so forbidden to eat the salt after meat was salted in it.}`,
  },
  'beer-hagolah': {
    '1#_': `Baraitta Chullin daf 113.`,
    '10#_': `Responsa of the Rashba siman 265.`,
    '11#_': `Rambam chapter 6 of Forbidden Foods law 10.`,
    '12#_': `From that of Rav Dimi of Neharde'a Chullin daf 113, and likewise the Rambam.`,
    '13#_': `Tur and the Rashba.`,
    '14#_': `There.`,
    '15#_': `Tur, and Beit Yosef wrote that his words are correct in their reason; and furthermore that if it were forbidden they would not have been silent about it in the Gemara.`,
    '16#_': `Tur, and likewise in Hagahot Maimoniyot in the name of Semak — as if all the salt were blood, for we hold a piece becomes nevelah in other prohibitions (the Rav ruled thus but not for his reason, for he holds a piece does not become nevelah in other prohibitions but because we do not know how much blood was absorbed in the salt — and see in Shach and Beit Yosef end of siman 105) (°) explanation: all that is in the pot.`,
    '17#_': `Semag and according to the explanation Beit Yosef explained of his words.`,
    '18#_': `Tur in the name of one authority, and to this agreed his father the Rosh and Hagahot Maimoniyot in the name of the Raavad and the Mordechai in the name of Rashbam and in the name of Rashi of Lunel.`,
    '19#_': `Tur in the name of the Geonim, and likewise Shaarei Dura in their name.`,
    '2#_': `Mordechai there — for as we say, through the pressure of the knife it absorbs — there daf 8; here too it expels through the pressure of the knife (and Maharil and hagahot there and Shach Devarim).`,
    '20#_': `The Rosh in a responsum in the name of Maharam, and likewise the Mordechai in their name and in the name of Or Zarua.`,
    '21#_': `Terumat HaDeshen siman 160 and Hagahot Shaarei Dura in the name of Maharam.`,
    '3#_': `Tur in the name of one authority, and to this agreed the Rosh; and so is the opinion of HaShilta; and likewise in Shaarei Dura.`,
    '4#_': `There in the name of one authority and Hagahot Maimoniyot in the name of Semak and hagahot of Shach Devarim.`,
    '5#_': `Rabbenu Yerucham, and he concludes; and perhaps this is the Rambam's intent in chapter 6 of Forbidden Foods.`,
    '6#_': `Tur in the name of his father the Rosh, from the dictum of Shmuel daf 113.`,
    '7#_': `There in the name of the Rashba, and likewise in HaShilta.`,
    '8#_': `There in the name of his father the Rosh, from that which Shmuel stated there and from that in Menachot daf 21 and as the text reads; and likewise for a pot.`,
    '9#_': `The Rashba in a responsum.`,
  },
  'mateh-yehonatan': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 69 seif 1) The Mechaber's wording "and if after he rinsed he cut each piece into two," etc. This law is from the Mordechai, and his proof from that stated in chapter 1 of Chullin that through the pressure of the knife it absorbs — even according to the one who says the place of slaughter is cold, likewise here through the pressure of the knife it expels. He challenges: did he not himself rule above that even according to the one who says the place of slaughter is cold — i.e. the place of slaughter is not completely hot but nevertheless is somewhat hot and through pressure it absorbs — not so here that it is completely cold; because of the pressure of the knife he says it does not expel. One may say that here, since it need only expel from meat that is soft, it is different. One may say that the pressure of the knife alone also expels, and his proof is thus: as in hot somewhat the pressure of the knife expels completely what was absorbed, likewise here that it is not absorbed so much in it, the pressure of the knife suffices to expel even when completely cold.`,
    '10#_': `(there seif 14) Meat that remained, etc. And the same law if cooked without melicha. Shach s.k. 59 requires further study, for below we hold, etc. — see his words at length. One may say why we say nullified in majority: since we can rely on tasting whether blood taste or not, wherever we can rely on tasting, not nullified in majority; therefore must be that they already tasted and said no taste, or that at cooking time there was sixty against the unsalted piece and Rama goes according to his view that piece forbidden even with sixty; afterward that piece mixed with another and he says nullified, for no taste at all since there is sixty; if so also Shach who does not hold if they cook will impart taste — we see no taste at all, all taste left; only that piece forbidden for blood spreading place to place, and for that there is no concern if she cooks will impart taste.`,
    '11#_': `(there seif 15) Soiled meat, etc., they say to eat it only roasted, etc. And although below seif 18 that if pickled in tzir it never comes out through roasting, and what is written here that it comes out through roasting — one may distinguish: there, since already expelled through melicha, the expulsion did not come through tzir only absorption; therefore we say absorbed so strongly it does not come out through roasting; Rama holds not to distinguish in such a case.`,
    '12#_': `(there seif 16 in hagahah) Or even without perforation if the meat already remained, etc. Shach s.k. 68 challenged in the name of Derishah that it should be forbidden because of pickling, and for resolution, certainly regarding salting in non-perforated vessel, etc.; but regarding melicha forever salt and tzir do not expel from vessel — appears even if meat was above beforehand, etc. — Derishah's challenge stands. Perhaps Mechaber's view even soiled permitted to roast because soaked and blood mixed in water — not found in any posek; perhaps Mechaber even soiled, since soaked and blood mixed in water, not visible blood; requires further study. Perhaps Derishah challenges: behold Rosh in chapter 25 taught if idolaters made cheese in their molds and salted in them, not forbidden, like pure salted and impure auxiliary — Rosh's proofs that salt has no power to expel from vessel, but absorption through melicha he did not deal with; if no power to expel, how wrote Rosh "wood not forbidden," etc. — must say Rosh holds only where much absorption through cooking is there power to expel; if not absorbed in vessel only through melicha, no power to expel; therefore Rosh permitted cheese in idolater molds because no absorption to expel; Shach disagrees on Rosh here.`,
    '13#_': `(there seif 20) Meat that was salted and remained, etc. Taz s.k. 4 holds that after measure of melicha it is no longer called boiling and even tzir is not boiling; in my opinion it appears from Rosh that at least tzir is boiling even after measure of melicha — in Rashi's incident the reason not to forbid tzir is only mohal, etc., and although salt on meat forbidden because blood mixed in it, therefore must rinse meat well, etc. — either blood in salt dried inside and has no power to exit, or power ceased because it expelled and salt no longer considered boiling; hastily one may answer the latter: why say above that after measure melicha not blood only mohal, permitted because no boiling at all; nevertheless after measure tzir is boiling, therefore say tzir is only mohal and nullifies boiling power of tzir on meat that exited during measure of melicha; therefore proved not like Rama and Taz as written.`,
    '14#_': `(there in hagahah) And some permit cutting with a knife, etc. There is melicha for vessels to absorb; challenged: we hold melicha for vessels to absorb! Answer: here reason is it slides off; not relying on sliding alone, for when meat salted and rinsed fell on meat that did not remain measure, forbidden — therefore no melicha, etc., meaning even to absorb not so much absorption as boiling, therefore sliding reason says did not absorb at all. Rama needed view that no melicha for vessels.`,
    '2#_': `(Shach s.k. 3) However the Ran's opinion in chapter 1 of Chullin, etc., that if he cut it afterward into two he need not return and rinse it, etc. However one may say the Ran goes according to his reason, etc. — end from Shach's words. And at first glance it is difficult, for then the Ran's challenge returns to its place against the Mordechai, and one must say the Mordechai holds therefore three knives are needed lest he cut forbidden fats and afterward meat and rely on the butcher's rinsing and salt the meat without rinsing; if so, for visible blood on it only as much as a peel is forbidden, unlike forbidden fat that is forbidden throughout, for it spreads — requires further study.`,
    '3#_': `(there in Rama's hagahah) Permitted to return and soak, etc. Shach wrote s.k. 10, and nevertheless b'dieved permitted, for it did not become pickled, etc., and Magen Avraham in laws of Pesach disagrees and holds that even b'dieved forbidden because what was absorbed is slight before the spoilage; if so, according to Magen Avraham, one must say that immediately when he removed the meat he rinsed in, the meat is immediately pickled from above; but if he placed meat in it a moment after removing the meat he rinsed in, the meat is permitted — this is forced. It appears to me that this certainly applies to pickling from above that is like cooking — not that very moment at the end of above causes absorption, but an hour or two before the above begins and is not finished until end of twenty-four hours. And if they put water or something else in the pot within the above in which meat was cooked and remained there above, and afterward puts forbidden fat after the above of the first cooking, or even other prohibitions — for those poskim all is forbidden since at the start of absorption it was within above, etc. — if so the absorbed joins with meat pickled in it, it is meat in milk and forbidden even if not meat in milk — long reasoning on pickling.`,
    '4#_': `(seif 9 in hagahah) Even if placed only in a kli sheni, etc. Since there is salt and tzir, etc., and even though we hold melicha requires cooking like ox meat, etc., salt does not hasten to cook, etc., and one may challenge the Rosh in chapter Kirah brought in Taz s.k. 23 who wrote that if old salt, perhaps forbidden, etc., and even if salted anew, although the meat does not cook, nevertheless the salt and moisture and blood in it cooked, etc. — how does he say that salting in it cooked? Melicha requires cooking like ox meat! One must distinguish: where he says cooking required, that is salt in its strength and in the eye; but if it dissolved somewhat on the meat and its power ceased, it cooks properly and also hastens to cook through the power of salt and tzir even in kli sheni. What Taz wrote "his words are astonishing," etc. — not necessarily compelled; one may say the Rosh deals with Shabbat, and what he concludes that it returns and absorbs, etc., is to say the meat is forbidden because of cooking on Shabbat — if it had not absorbed the moisture of blood cooked on Shabbat the meat would be permitted, for the meat does not cook at all in kli rishon; if so no prohibition was made in this meat at all, therefore he says the moisture of blood that cooked is absorbed and the meat is forbidden. What Rama wrote in Teshuvot HaRosh, etc., Rabbenu Yerucham permits unsalted meat — one who examines Rabbenu Yerucham Netiv 15 part 5 sees explicitly he deals when there is no water, dry stuck to vessel walls; but if there is water, one may say he agrees forbidden as the Rosh said.`,
    '5#_': `(seif 10) An idolater serving in an Israelite's house, etc. Shach s.k. 42, and according to his words one must say he does not disagree with siman 134, etc. In haste it is not comparable to there where presumption of prohibition was established, unlike here where there was no prohibition on the meat at all, and before explaining even blood was permitted; nevertheless since presumption was established in blood, properly called presumption of prohibition; what compelled this was necessarily called impossible, otherwise why need the reason of fear and masiach lefi tumo — otherwise permitted as rabbinic doubt leniently; nevertheless one must say presumption of prohibition, and where impossible, only rabbinic doubt leniently.`,
    '6#_': `(there seif 12) [Hagahah of Rav the third, etc., R' Shlomo HaKohen, Vilna. And this is his wording.] Behold in this law it is explained in Beit Yosef in the name of Semak who wrote another reason because he is concerned for cleanliness; and Tur omitted this reason and one must understand why; it appears at first glance difficult: why does the reason of cleanliness help — rinsing once suffices for cleanliness! However for the law we require two rinsings as poskim wrote from "and rinse it very well." However in truth Semak goes according to his view that b'dieved one rinsing suffices as in Hagahot Maimoniyot on his name brought in Beit Yosef. However Tur may hold that even b'dieved two rinsings are required, as appears from Hagahot Ashiri that it is required even b'dieved, therefore he omitted this, for according to his view this reason does not help at all. And further: there is concern that even though he rinsed, perhaps he placed the meat in the vessel before putting water, and Tur's view on this is that even b'dieved forbidden, for he wrote that if he salted in coarse salt and did not shake or rinse in water first, not forbidden because water nullifies the power of salt, etc. — implying only when there is water in the vessel permitted b'dieved; if not, not. Therefore he omitted it. Unlike Semak who may hold that even if no water at all in the vessel, also not forbidden b'dieved as Rama wrote in hagahah in the name of Shach Devarim and Orchot Hayim, and the reason of cleanliness helps for his view. End hagahah of Rav R' Shlomo HaKohen.]`,
    '7#_': `(there seif 12) He shall not cook it, etc. And if they cooked, permitted. Shach s.k. 51, and he disagrees with liver in siman 73 where melicha does not help because of abundance of blood and presumably all was expelled in roasting and what did not expel in roasting will not expel in cooking; but here melicha does not help because blood already dried inside; according to his words if liver remained three days without melicha, also forbidden to cook after roasting. But Tosafot explained the reason permitting cooking liver after roasting: liver is all blood and the Torah permitted it, forbidden only rabbinically; and since it became roasted, even if blood remains, permitted; if so according to this reason even if it remained three days without melicha, also permitted to cook after roasting.`,
    '8#_': `(there) And one should not leave meat three days, etc., for we are concerned lest they cook it. He challenges from a chicken with sixty treifot doubts permitted to leave only one day and we are not concerned for mishap. Some want to say it is different there because slaughter is required and it is a major act remembered within the time it is treifah; but this is not so, for permitted to leave even eggs one day without this reason; one must say that regarding sixty definite prohibitions with no heter, people separate themselves and mishap does not apply; but here, since permitted to roast, since there is heter for roasting, people do not separate and properly concerned for mishap. [And as support, from beginning of siman 68, see Shach — end hagahah of Maharash HaKohen.]`,
    '9#_': `(seif 13) Meat that was salted and a doubt whether salted within three days — permitted. One may be lenient according to what Taz wrote s.k. 4, incident of a woman who cooked meat and forgot if she salted, and she was permitted because blood in cooking is rabbinic and sixty rabbinic leniently; if so, even a doubt perhaps not salted at all is also permitted, for blood that was salted is only rabbinic and doubt leniently; if so why say specifically within three days permitted because it is stringency of Geonim — even without this reason permitted because rabbinic doubt leniently. One may say: since we hold melicha forbids only as much as a peel, salt has no more power than a peel, and we do not say blood that was salted is definitely rabbinic; therefore without the Geonic stringency I would say sixty stringently; and what Taz wrote sixty leniently is specifically after cooking. Therefore one may answer what Maharshal challenged on Rabbenu Yerucham that he need not give reason that blood in cooking is rabbinic, for from melicha too it would be d'oraisa forbidden — according to my words one may say Rabbenu Yerucham wants a reason that the idolater is believed even on salting meat, i.e. if the idolater says he salted, the law is thus; if so compelled to reason it is like blood in cooking, for if because of melicha more than a peel, not blood that was salted; requires further study. [Editor: his words are astonishing — if so how say because melicha is like boiling it is blood in cooking; nevertheless not boiling only peel measure; also what he answers here in doubt that remained three days deals before cooking — nevertheless will be rabbinic doubt after cooking; one must say because nullifying prohibition that was initially d'oraisa and wants to cook and make rabbinic doubt — end editor note on resolving Maharshal.]`,
  },
  'pitchei-teshuva': {
    '1#_': `To rinse the meat. See in Beit Yosef who wrote that in cold places and in a time of great cold they put the water in the sun until its chill departs and afterward rinse the meat in it, for otherwise, on the contrary, through the cold in the water it hardens on the meat.`,
    '10#_': `Even b'dieved. Be'er Heitev and in Chokhmat Adam siman 36 wrote regarding an incident where they salted meat in salt mixed with groats a third or quarter — forbidden, since they are accustomed otherwise that there is empty space, and when the place of groats joins, there will be much empty space and in that place it is eaten because of its salt, and this hinders b'dieved according to all — therefore if not yet remained the measure of melicha, he should rinse and return to salt with proper salt, for forbidden to salt while salt and blood are on it; and if remained the measure of melicha, he should rinse slightly and salt, for if he rinses well the holes of expulsion close and the place that was salted properly absorbs from the place not salted; also he salts on a clean place, not on that vessel in which he salted the first time — see there.`,
    '11#_': `If not for need. See in responsum Rav Meshulam siman 6 that there is no difference whether the salted side is above or below, for certainly the salt draws the blood and pulls it to the place where it is, whether from below to above or from above to below.`,
    '12#_': `B'dieved. Be'er Heitev — and it appears that if not yet cooked, even though rinsed, he returns and salts it. And see in Dagul Meryavah who wrote that specifically if not remained only the measure of a mil; but if remained more than half an hour, one may be lenient after already rinsed and need not lose salt and salt again — see there.`,
    '13#_': `For honoring guests. He wrote in Solet LaMinchah general rule 15 in the name of Turei Even: where honoring guests is mentioned, that is such as Torah scholars and men of good reputation and orphans, or a poor man of good family, or wealthy men fit to honor who do not do evil — not the wicked or misers who pass through for prey and food; they are in the category of charity — see there. He further wrote that called "guests" only those who lodged in his house, but not when he invited his friend. And he disagrees on this and concludes that l'chatchila he should not invite his friend if he knows he must be lenient regarding rinsing or melicha; but if he already invited him, then one may be lenient as because of other guests — end of his words, see there.`,
    '14#_': `In fruit juice. Be'er Heitev. And see in responsum Radbaz, the new ones, siman 37, that there it states explicitly that even in the first rinsing permitted in fruit juice — and see there further.`,
    '15#_': `In a kli sheni. Be'er Heitev [and see Peri Megadim above siman 68 in Shaarei Tzedek s.k. 26 who wrote a question: three pots boiling, kli rishon from one — he poured on a quarter of the bird before final rinsing, and there is another quarter that was rinsed from its melicha, etc.; answer: what they flayed through irui is inferior, etc. — see there. And see in Beit Yehudah there who disagrees and what he wrote] in the name of Taz — incident of a woman, etc. And see in Sha'ar HaMelekh chapter 10 of laws of mikvaot law 6 in general rules of rabbinic doubts, rule 7, what he challenged on Taz, and what I wrote in siman 110 in laws of double doubt, letter chaf, and so on. And in responsum Shaviv, the Gaon R' Shabsai Katz, manuscript, wrote that he permitted in an incident that came before him — a woman who forgot if she salted the other side, for even Shach who forbids if salted on one side after rinsed, and likewise Nekudat HaKesef is stringent if she forgot if she salted — one may say in such a case permitted, for there the reason is a major act how she forgot; but in such a case, on the contrary, since she salted one side, the matter is habitual that she does — end of his words. And see in responsum Toldot Yitzchak end of siman 80. And see in responsum Orach L'Chaim, Panim Me'irot part 1 siman 11, asked regarding a woman who sent meat to her husband for Yom Kippur and did not mention in the letter if she salted the meat, and the husband cooked the meat without melicha — if permitted to eat the meat; he showed face to here and to there and concluded his heart wavered on this matter to permit the meat unless some indication joined proving she certainly salted, such as the messenger could not come except close to Shabbat so there was no time to salt and cook for Shabbat — then we say she certainly salted before she sent — see there. And see in responsum Shaviv part 2 siman 69 who disagrees with Peri Megadim in what he wrote such as the messenger could not come, etc., for this is not an indication that proves, perhaps the sender's intent was to salt some time before honoring Shabbat and to insulate it close to Shabbat or to roast on a spit through a little melicha that needs not so much time; and otherwise, since it is something that can be clarified, there is no side at all to permit through doubt, and even in double doubt one should be stringent — and see there what he further wondered on Peri Megadim in that responsum.`,
    '16#_': `To salt in it a second time. In Chamudei Daniel siman 39 wrote that salt of arnunit that dried is forbidden to salt meat in it, and even b'dieved one should forbid, for it is inferior to salt in which meat was salted, since it became pickled from above in tzir — see there. [And see in responsum Shavot Yaakov part 3 siman 66 regarding water in which meat was rinsed that spilled on salt — what to do with the salt; he ruled that he should leave it to dry and afterward may salt meat in it to expel its blood, but to put in food forbidden; and if it is much salt he should not leave in his house lest they err to put it in food — see there.]`,
    '17#_': `And one of these suffices. Thus Semak: if an idolater serves in an Israelite's house and placed meat in a pot and we do not know if they rinsed it — not believed in masiach lefi tumo according to Peri To'ar; and all the more so if they know Jewish customs; and if there is a lad or maiden who knows the matter of rinsing, or an Israelite going in and out — permitted because he is afraid, etc. — end of his words. And all the great later authorities strained his wording as Taz wrote. And see in responsum Noda Biyehudah part Even HaEzer end of siman 31 who wrote to explain Semak's words with good reason: it is clear that the idolater is believed on a rabbinic prohibition where there are presumptive grounds, even if not masiach lefi tumo; and proof from what is written below siman 187 seif 8 that if it is recognizable the remedies helped, one may rely even on an idolater even if not masiach lefi tumo, and there it is rabbinic prohibition; and since so, here blood that was salted is rabbinic. And behold, if he knows their Jewish customs, called presumptive grounds to act like the custom since he serves in an Israelite's house; if so, three distinctions in the matter: if masiach lefi tumo — even if he does not know their customs and there are no presumptive grounds, believed because of masiach lefi tumo; if he knows their customs — presumptive grounds and believed even if not masiach lefi tumo; and possible that presumptive grounds with testimony of an idolater even if not masiach lefi tumo is preferable to masiach lefi tumo where there are no presumptive grounds; therefore Semak wrote the wording "and all the more so." And where there is a minor of understanding going in and out and the idolater knows their customs — permitted and not required even that the idolater say at all that he rinsed; and without his statement we say presumably from fear he certainly rinsed; unlike if no Israelite going in and out — even if he knows their customs, nevertheless not permitted presumptively, only if he says explicitly that he rinsed, even if not masiach lefi tumo. And with this Semak's wording is explained well — end of his words, see there.`,
    '18#_': `Was cooked. In the notebook of Orach L'Chaim the Rav found written: Agudah says R' — permitted to cook meat without melicha in Tiberias hot springs. [There in Shulchan Aruch: requires sixty in the dish. See in Birkei Yosef who brought in the name of one great authority that this is when they put the meat in water that is not boiling; but if they put it in boiling water, it is chalita and permitted b'dieved without sixty as explained below siman 73 seif 2, etc.; and he weighed and examined somewhat and concludes that if the matter is clear the water was very boiling and there is constraint such as erev Shabbat, one may be lenient — see there. And Eshel Avraham there s.k. 10 — requires further study.]`,
    '19#_': `That piece. See in responsum Shemesh Tzedakah, Yoreh De'ah, siman 31, regarding fat that was not salted and was cooked — if one must estimate also against all the fat or not, since there is not so much blood in fat — see there. And it appears he omitted the words of Turei Even and Shach below siman 75 s.k. 8 where it is explained there that requires sixty against all the fat — see there.`,
    '2#_': `The butcher rinsed it. Be'er Heitev. And see Chokhmat Adam who wrote that one may be lenient when he rinsed while the blood was still warm, even with a slight rinsing. And what he wrote "however if we know," etc. — see in Neta Sorek that I wrote that this is specifically in a place where the custom is that butchers soak in water, but otherwise we do not say so.`,
    '20#_': `Against it. Be'er Heitev. And what he wrote in the name of R' Shlomo HaLevi — and see on this in responsum Ma'il Shmuel siman 11 who disagrees with the ruling of R' Shlomo HaLevi — and see in responsum Shemesh Tzedakah Yoreh De'ah siman 38 in hagahah from the author's son who rejected his words and raised like the view of R' Shlomo HaLevi.`,
    '21#_': `Meat that remained. See in Be'er Heitev of the Rav Maharit who wrote below siman 75 s.k. 5 in the name of responsum Shaviv to be lenient with goose fat that was not rinsed within three days. And see in responsum Noda Biyehudah part Yoreh De'ah siman 25 who disagreed with Shaviv here, who permitted the forbidden, and wrote that even according to Shaviv, specifically fat that was stripped; but if the fat was not yet stripped from the goose, he too agrees that even the fat alone is forbidden to cook; and one who ruled to permit even the fat alone deserves rebuke with nezifah; and if the act was already done and he stripped the fat and cooked, the vessels and all the mixture are forbidden — see there. Also in responsum Maor HaGolah Rabbi Akiva Eiger siman 44 disagrees with Shaviv on this. And see in Chokhmat Adam who also wrote that with fat the law is like with meat, except he wrote that one may permit through cooking in water with sixty after melicha, since in great loss we rely on the Mechaber in seif 11, and the water that absorbed prohibition he pours out, and this is not nullifying prohibition because the fat will return and absorb from the water — this is not nullification of prohibition for absorption comes after nullification; and further our intent is to melt. Fat is somewhat similar to siman 84 seif 63; but to mix it with other fat until sixty appears forbidden — end of his words. (And it appears that in this manner even with meat permitted; however for the second reason Chokhmat Adam wrote it is not nullifying prohibition for our intent is only to melt the fat — possible that with meat forbidden for the reason Taz wrote in siman 137 s.k. 4 that not permitted without intent for that except where impossible otherwise — see there; and likewise with meat possible through roasting, but with fat impossible otherwise for in roasting the fat is consumed.) And see in responsum Rabbi Akiva Eiger siman 38 who raised to be lenient in this with great loss — namely, that initially he salts the fat as required, and after melicha and rinsing he mixes with other fat that was salted and rinsed as required — see there for his reason.`,
    '3#_': `He cut. Shach — specifically with a knife, for there is pressure. And see Chokhmat Adam who wrote that if a cat ate a little of the piece, he need not rinse the place of the eating — requires further study.`,
    '4#_': `Into two. And if he did not cut into two, only cut a little — Peri Megadim in Shaarei Tzedek s.k. 3 wrote that permitted b'dieved, for there is sixty; and even though not salted there, for the Mordechai, nevertheless in the remaining place without salt Shach permits, and likewise permitted — end of his words. And according to what he wrote below s.k. 15 in the name of Chamudei Daniel, permitted only when a little place remains without salt; but if much remains, even b'dieved forbidden, for in that place it is eaten because of its salt — it appears that here too forbidden and its law is as salted on one side — requires further study for practical law.`,
    '5#_': `To soak it up to half an hour. If it soaked in a vessel in water and half the piece was above the water — Peri Megadim in the Introduction wrote that according to the reason to soften, permitted, for the water enters the meat and the whole piece softens — see there; and if so, rinsing the side that was above the water suffices, for according to the other reasons he need not soak half an hour.`,
    '6#_': `Full of ice. Be'er Heitev — what he wrote in the name of Maharshal, meat that hardened, etc. And see in Levit Chen on the Torah, parashat Acharei Mot, who wrote regarding meat that was salted and remained the measure of melicha and was rinsed and afterward ice was found in its middle — it does not help for this meat to return and salt it again after thawing, since the meat around it thawed at the time of melicha and expelled all its blood, melicha will no longer expel the blood inside like in the brain in siman 75, and there is no remedy for this meat except to roast it — end of his words, see there. And see in responsum Har HaKarmel, Yoreh De'ah part, siman 12, who rejected this reason but forbids for another reason — that part of the meat that already remained in its salt properly and its blood exited and was rinsed and the holes of expulsion closed — when they salt again it returns and absorbs from this inner meat — and again he raised that also for this reason one need not forbid — see there. And see in responsum Givat Shaul siman 16 that his view in this law is that if not yet rinsed there is remedy to permit by rinsing beforehand only to remove the moisture of tzir, and then certainly melicha a second time helps even not in a place of great loss; but if already rinsed well, no remedy except roasting; and if already salted again after rinsed as proper complete rinsing, one must forbid even for roasting — see there well. And see in Dagul Meryavah what he wrote on this. And what Be'er Heitev further wrote in the name of Mateh Yehonatan, meat that soaked in water and hardened, etc. — see on this in responsum Devar Shmuel siman 573 that his view is to forbid even if not soaked in ice, only from above; and see in responsum Kiryat Chana siman 29 who raised like Mateh Yehonatan that ice is not considered like water, therefore if not remained in ice, only from above, certainly permitted; and even if remained so three days, also not clear to forbid — see there.`,
    '7#_': `And even if he did not remain in his salt. Be'er Heitev — specifically for a pot; but for roasting permitted without remaining, and likewise Rama below siman 76 seif 2. And see in Tiferet Moshe who wrote that permitted only when both — for greater stringency — he did not salt much and did not remain, and also Rama's view in siman 76 is explained thus — see there.`,
    '8#_': `And the same law if there was sixty. [Peri Megadim in Mishbetzot who explained Rama's words here that permitted through rinsing and melicha a second time specifically — see there. And see in Levushei Serad simanim 108–109 who disagrees and concludes that l'chatchila he shall rinse and salt again, and b'dieved if cooked without melicha a second time, permitted — see there.]`,
    '9#_': `Permitted to salt in it. See in Chamudei Daniel siman 10 who wrote on this: one must put salt on the meat that it be somewhat thick; and if coarse salt is available, forbidden to salt with fine salt even if he puts much, for the first part of the salt that drew near to the meat enters the meat and does not act in it. He further wrote that one must be careful that through grinding much of the salt becomes like flour, and when he salts with all the salt together, much of the meat does not receive from the fine salt; and regarding b'dieved it appears from his words that even if he salted all the meat with fine salt alone and did not add until somewhat thick, permitted — see there.`,
  },
  'rabbi-akiva-eiger-yd': {
    '1#_': `(Siman 69, Shach s.k. 1) The reason is so it softens. Explained in Beit Yosef that when the cold is great they put water in the sun until its chill departs before rinsing in them, for cold water hardens and does not soften — see above (siman 36 seif 4). And b'dieved if he rinsed in very cold water, one should discuss whether it helps to rinse again in lukewarm water to soften, or since the meat hardened through cold water it will no longer soften; and one may learn to permit from that of meat full of ice at the end of this seif. And if he salted after rinsing in cold water, it has remedy through rinsing and melicha a second time — from both sides: for the reason of visible blood, rinsing in cold water helps; for the reason of softening, rinsing and melicha a second time help. And if he did not rinse and salted again and cooked thus — requires further study for practical law, for according to Taz s.k. 10 and Shach s.k. 14, slight rinsing is permitted b'dieved for we rely on the reason of visible blood (or combined with the reason lest the salt dissolve) — likewise permitted. But if we explain that slight rinsing permitted refers to above that permitted with rinsing and melicha a second time (and this appears from what is written in hagahah "and the same if there was sixty against visible blood," and in this that for all reasons forbidden except for visible blood — if so, permitted without rinsing and melicha a second time and without great loss, it would be compelled that we hold the primary reason is visible blood and we are not concerned at all for other reasons; and behold in "he salted and did not rinse" that rinsing and melicha a second time in great loss helps — behold we rely to say the reason is not visible blood; and Rama's hagahah words refer to remedy of rinsing and melicha a second time) — if so, likewise when he rinsed in cold water one may say forbidden without rinsing and melicha a second time, and even if cooked and there is no great loss (Av Beit Din — see what my grandfather the third wrote on Shach below s.k. 14).`,
    '10#_': `(Shach s.k. 14) Still considered b'dieved. The conclusion of the later authorities is not so; rather Rama's intent is that with rinsing or with sixty, rinsing and melicha a second time help — for the reason of softening the second rinsing helps, and for the other reason the first rinsing helps. And if cooked through slight rinsing and melicha — requires further study; one may say permitted since there are two sides to permit, namely the reason lest salt dissolve, or because of visible blood slight rinsing helps; and without rinsing at all, only with sixty — one should forbid, since permitted only to taste because of visible blood, and we do not judge taste for substance, since in great loss they permit with rinsing and melicha a second time — we rely the reason is softening or lest salt dissolve; however in great loss requires further study. And see what I wrote in the margin above at the beginning of the seif that the implication is one law — slight rinsing with the law of sixty against visible blood; and since with sixty certainly impossible to be lenient without great loss and to rely on visible blood — likewise with slight rinsing, since Rama weighed one scale.`,
    '11#_': `(Seif 4 in hagahah) Even b'dieved fat salted on one side permitted. See below (siman 75).`,
    '12#_': `(there) Within twelve hours. Also with a bird it is so — so wrote in Darkei Moshe. And see in Peri Megadim siman 70 in Shach Devarim.`,
    '13#_': `(there) He returns and salts the other side. In my opinion one should examine: what helps melicha of the other side? For the side already salted and remained the measure of melicha — the power of salt ceased, and if so it is as if not salted; if so, now in the second melicha also only salted on one side! It was fitting to salt the whole again on both sides, for it is clear we do not say that salting one side helps for thickness, for if the piece were cut in two it would be permitted — this is not implied; rather we say salt's power enters only a little into thickness sometimes like the thickness of a finger, etc.; or one may say the first melicha expelled blood from thickness of a finger, salt's power ceased, and now when salting the other side it also expels like thickness of a finger and the middle remains in its blood — requires further study.`,
    '14#_': `(Taz s.k. 15) Since there is heter otherwise in roasting. In my opinion: if so, after twelve hours why not salt? Let us also say since this matter of measure of tzir expulsion twelve hours is not agreed — the primary view is more like poskim that its measure is twenty-four hours; here too, since there is heter otherwise in a place of great loss, rely on them that measure of tzir expulsion is twenty-four hours.`,
    '15#_': `(Beit Yosef) And I wondered on Rama in siman 75. In my opinion requires further study: there melicha on the inner side certainly does not help since there is no blood there, therefore if cooked, even with great loss forbidden because of blood in the fat; but to return and salt is permitted since in fat there is only a little blood; but here for Rashba that melicha on one side helps — no blood remains at all, and preferable to that below; and for Maharai that melicha on one side does not help — much blood remains and inferior to that below; therefore in great loss we rely on Rashba and there is no blood at all; but without great loss we are concerned for Maharai that melicha on one side does not help and consequently much blood in the piece and returning to salt does not help — clear with God's help.`,
    '16#_': `(Shach s.k. 3) And the same in fruit juice. Not understood: if from reasoning fruit juice is like rainwater, why need Ran's words? For otherwise we know rainwater helps and need not examine specifically!`,
    '17#_': `(Beit Yosef at end) And requires further study. In my opinion one may say Turei Even's intent is certainly Tosafot's view that both first and final rinsing are permitted in fruit juice; and since Tosafot hold so, it is reasonable without compulsion, for we find no one who disagrees; and this is for final rinsing; but for first rinsing there is no proof from Tosafot, for perhaps they hold like poskim that first rinsing's reason is visible blood, therefore permitted in fruit juice; but for softening one may say fruit juice does not help — requires further study.`,
    '18#_': `(Seif 9) Meat that was salted. With fat in such a case, presumably there is sixty against blood and salt — Peri Megadim below siman 75 in Shach Devarim s.k. 9 in the name of Kenat Hagedolah.`,
    '19#_': `(there) Requires sixty. And likewise if it soaked in water from time to time — Turei Chaim part 1 law 4 in the name of Orchot Hayim.`,
    '2#_': `(Seif 1 in hagahah) It is as if not rinsed at all. It is difficult for me: behold without rinsing at all, if he salted and cooked, even b'dieved and with great loss forbidden; and in this that he cut a piece, if he did not return and rinse them, permitted with great loss, for in great loss we rely on the reason of softening as explained in seif 2; and for that reason of softening he need not return and rinse as Shach wrote s.k. 3.`,
    '20#_': `(Taz s.k. 19) For the salt became all of it. It is astonishing: behold the Mechaber holds we do not say the piece itself becomes nevelah in other prohibitions; rather certainly the reason is we do not know how much was absorbed — as Shach wrote s.k. 34.`,
    '21#_': `(Beit Yosef) And one should not act so. This is astonishing: behold Maharshal disagreed and if so the knife requires rinsing afterward or sticking in the ground if tzir dried on it (HaAroch) — I found that Maharshal challenged thus in Minchat Yitzchak.`,
    '3#_': `(there in hagahah) Only rinsed well. And if he rinsed well on one side and on the other side slight rinsing — one may say that suffices: for softening one may say that through one side also it softened; for visible blood slight rinsing helps — requires further study for practical law.`,
    '4#_': `(Shulchan Aruch) Law of meat full of ice. If after it was salted and rinsed ice is found in its middle — see in Levit Chen on the Torah, parashat Acharei Mot.`,
    '5#_': `(Taz s.k. 5) And specifically a wet matter in cold. It implies that dry cold is permitted, for the eater rinses; and even in something not customary to rinse we are not concerned lest he be negligent and eat without rinsing, since b'dieved we judge that the vessel was rinsed well (for from Beit Yosef when he used it boiling, permitted b'dieved) — therefore we are not concerned for negligence — see Taz (siman 91 s.k. 1); and here preferable, for b'dieved we rely that he acted properly, for we judge the vessel rinsed properly — not required to rinse the food.`,
    '6#_': `(Seif 2) And some forbid. A chicken from outside — there is no visible blood — Peri Megadim below siman 72 in Shach s.k. 4.`,
    '7#_': `(Taz s.k. 8) That impossible even melicha for roasting. It is difficult for me: for if so, even if he rinsed first, melicha for roasting should be forbidden, for it expels a little and returns and absorbs, for it is not busy expelling much since salted only a little.`,
    '8#_': `(Taz s.k. 9) Even if remained in his salt. It is difficult for me: for at first glance, specifically when it remained; but without remaining, since there is heter in roasting as below (siman 76 seif 2), not called great loss according to Taz nearby s.k. 15.`,
    '9#_': `(Seif 2 in hagahah) And the same if there was sixty in the piece. Nevertheless requires peeling.`,
  },
  'kaf-hachayim': {
    '1#_': `(1) [Seif 1] One must rinse the meat before melicha. Regarding blood that was salted or cooked — whether its prohibition is d'oraisa or d'rabbanan — there is dispute among the great ones: Rashi's view in Chullin daf 109a at the end, s.v. halev, that blood that was cooked one is liable for karet; and likewise daf 123a s.v. hakafah — see there; and Beit Yosef wrote in siman 87 that this appears to be Rambam's view. And likewise Peleti end of siman 1 that Rif and Rambam are like Rashi. And likewise the Mechaber siman 1 that Rambam chapter 6 of Forbidden Foods law 6 is like Rashi — see there. And likewise Radbaz part 1 siman 199. But Tosafot there Chullin 109a s.v. halev and 123a s.v. hakafah wrote that blood that was cooked one does not transgress. And likewise Peri Chadash siman 1 that Tosafot, Rashba, Ran, and Ra'ah plainly hold blood that was salted is forbidden only rabbinically; and Beit Yosef the Mechaber there, and wrote that so is Ritva's view in his commentary to Chullin daf 111 — see there. And so appears Beit Yosef's view who wrote in this siman on Semak's words regarding an idolater serving in an Israelite's house, etc., that the idolater is not believed except with a woman's testimony — that is for a d'oraisa prohibition, but for a rabbinic prohibition believed in masiach lefi tumo; and this too is rabbinic prohibition, for since melicha is like boiling, it is blood that was cooked and one does not transgress — end of his words. And likewise at the end of this siman on what Maharshal wondered on Rashba, Beit Yosef wrote "it appears to me there is no place for wonder, for since blood that was salted one does not transgress, they were not concerned to decree on it" — end of his words; and Peri Chadash agreed at end of siman 1. However Hagahot Reuven siman 1 wrote: it appears primary to be stringent like Rashi and Rambam that blood that was salted or cooked is d'oraisa; and he further wrote that even according to those who say rabbinic, Rashba in Torat HaBayit daf 75 wrote that since the essence of blood is d'oraisa, they were stringent in its doubt as Torah law — see there. It follows this matter is a second dispute; therefore it appears that if there are further sides to be lenient, one may rely on those who say blood that was salted or cooked is rabbinic and follow its doubt leniently. And see further below siman 87 seif 50.`,
    '10#_': `(10) There in hagahah. Or he removed hooves of the legs after rinsing. But if he did not remove them, all is considered one piece of meat and rinsing them as they are suffices, since there is no visible blood. Shach s.k. 4; Shaarei De'ah seif 4; Mekor Chaim seif 7. And regarding if they were salted thus without removing hooves whether what is inside the hooves is forbidden — see above siman 68 seif 8 and our words there.`,
    '11#_': `(11) There. He must return and rinse them. And not required that it soften, for already the meat softened. Mishnat Zekenim seif 3; Chochmat Adam seif 4; Beit Yitzchak part 1 in Amud HaZahav seif 10; margin of Maharsha; Zivchei Tzedek seif 7 — and when removing hooves after melicha, need only rinse the place of the hooves.`,
    '12#_': `(12) There in hagahah. And if he did not do so, it is as if not rinsed at all. And if he cut after rinsing into two, or a piece not rinsed only on one side — permitted b'dieved with great loss. Shaarei De'ah seif 3 — and this is according to what is written in seif 4 regarding a piece salted only on one side the Shulchan Aruch permits and in hagahah some forbid; and the later authorities wrote there that with great loss one may permit if already cooked — as explained — and in our words there. And the same if he removed hooves of legs after rinsing: estimate if above the hooves there is corresponding to what was in the hooves, it is as a piece salted on one side.`,
    '13#_': `(13) However if he cut only a little, one may permit even without great loss, for we are not meticulous about this because of what Shach wrote s.k. 21. Shaarei De'ah there; margin of Maharsha. And this is when he cut a little, meaning a small amount; but if he cut much, it is as cutting each piece in two, and as salted on one side one may not permit except with great loss as written in the previous seif; with this the doubt Pitchei Teshuvah wrote in seif 4 is resolved.`,
    '14#_': `(14) There in hagahah. Rinsing meat l'chatchila he should be careful to soak up to half an hour, etc. — according to Ran's reason that rinsing is so the meat softens and expels its blood. Biur HaGra seif 4; and in Orchot Hayim shaar 1 seif 1 wrote more than half an hour, and likewise in Darkei Moshe seif 1, except because it is only custom he did not write here in hagahah to be precise to write more than half an hour.`,
    '15#_': `(15) There in hagahah. Rinsing meat l'chatchila he should soak up to an hour, etc.; and if the face of the piece on one side was not covered in soak-water, l'chatchila suffices also to rinse that side well, for through soaking of the body of the piece all softens well. Peri Megadim in the Introduction; Mekor Chaim seif 16; Pitchei Teshuvah seif 5; Zivchei Tzedek seif 12.`,
    '16#_': `(16) There in hagahah. And to rinse it well in the soak-water — meaning rinse until the waters become white without appearance of blood, as Shach wrote in siman 10 s.k. 21 in the name of Rashba and Ran. And likewise Chochmat Adam in this siman seif 1; and Zivchei Tzedek seif 9 wrote that in three times the waters whiten, except he wrote the main thing is rubbing well, for if he does not rub, perhaps even with rinsing three times the waters will not whiten. And likewise Ben Ish Chai in parashat Tazria seif 16 that presumably in three times the waters will be clean.`,
    '17#_': `(17) And Zivchei Tzedek wrote there that in Baghdad they do not practice soaking half an hour, rather rinse and rub well as the Shulchan Aruch states at the head of the seif that one must rinse meat before melicha, and did not write this of half an hour. And likewise Ben Ish Chai there that there are places that do not practice soaking meat as the Shulchan Aruch that did not mention soaking, only rinsing; and so they practice in Baghdad — see there. But in our holy city Jerusalem, Sephardim too practice like our teacher's words to soak meat half an hour, and possible that in all places they practice so and one should not change.`,
    '18#_': `(18) Meat that was soaked in a vessel in sea water — one sage arose to forbid, and it has no further remedy to salt it, since sea water is very salty, it is as meat salted in a non-perforated vessel; and Rav Moshe Mat part 1 siman 74 rejected his words and permitted for several reasons — see there. Shevet Sofer seif 1; Sdei Chemed seif 8.`,
    '19#_': `(19) There in hagahah. But if he did not soak, only rinsed well, it suffices for him. And accordingly it appears that for honoring guests or erev Shabbat when time is short, which is b'dieved as below seif 6 in hagahah, it suffices to rinse well by hand on all sides and to salt. Mekor Chaim seif 12. And likewise Chayei Adam general rule 30 seif 3: for great need where he has no time to soak half an hour, he rinses very very well in water and rubs well and returns and rinses in water a second time until the waters remain white. And likewise Darkei Teshuvah seif 22 in the name of several poskim; especially according to what is written above seif 15 that according to the Shulchan Aruch soaking is not required — if so, wherever there is need and no time, one may be lenient only to wash and rub the meat well until rinse-waters are white, and also see if there are hardened blood drops on the meat to remove them at the time of washing.`,
    '2#_': `(2) There. One must rinse the meat before melicha, as stated in chapter Kol HaBasar (Chullin 113a): Shmuel said meat does not leave its blood except if one salts it very well and rinses it very well. Rav Huna said: salt and rinse. In the baraita it taught: rinse, salt, and rinse — they do not disagree: this where they rinsed in the slaughterhouse, this where they did not rinse in the slaughterhouse — end of matter. And Ran wrote in the name of Ra'ah: the first rinsing must remove blood on the surface of the meat, for salt in its heat expels only blood inside the meat that is moist, but not what dried on its surface; therefore if he does not rinse it first, one should be concerned that after he leaves the meat to expel blood and tzir, the blood will melt and be absorbed — end of matter. And he wrote another reason: why they rinse at first — so the meat softens and its blood exits through melicha; for if it hardens on its surface, salt will not have power to expel its blood. And the reason for rinsing after melicha is to rinse the moisture of blood on the surface of the meat that comes from its expulsion — end of matter. And Mordechai wrote: first rinsing to remove filth of blood on the piece, for otherwise salt fills from it and again salt will not expel blood in the piece. Beit Yosef. And Beit Yosef, Taz s.k. 1, wrote: it appears they disagree on this — some forbid and some permit in seif 2 regarding salting without rinsing first — for some permit who hold the second reason of Ra'ah, and some forbid who hold Ra'ah's first reason — see there. And likewise Shach s.k. 1 that according to Ran and Mordechai it helps if he returns and salts again even after time — see there. And for practical law, see below in seif 2 — see there.`,
    '20#_': `(20) There in hagahah. But if he did not soak, only rinsed well, it suffices. And not required to trouble again to soak even if not yet salted; but with slight rinsing it does not suffice and he must return to rinse well. Shach s.k. 5; Peri Chadash seif 6; Lechem HaPanim seif 5; Lechem Panim in seif 1; Shaarei De'ah seif 5; Chochmat Adam seif 5; Mekor Chaim there; Beit Yitzchak in Amud HaZahav seif 6; Zivchei Tzedek seif 16.`,
    '21#_': `(21) But if he salted, even slight rinsing suffices b'dieved. Shach there; Peri Chadash there; Lechem HaPanim there; Lechem Panim there; Turei Da'at there; Chayei Adam general rule 32 seif 1; Beit Yitzchak there. And what Shach wrote "it suffices b'dieved" means even if not yet cooked, as Shach himself wrote s.k. 14. However Minchat Yitzchak on Turei Chaim general rule 5 seif 3 and general rule 15 seif 12 challenged Shach's words and wrote that permitted b'dieved with slight rinsing is after cooking; but if not cooked, only salted — since it has remedy in rinsing and melicha a second time, not called b'dieved — see there. And this is that they disagree on what is written in hagahah below seif 2 that if not rinsed only a little before salting, permitted b'dieved whether before cooking or after cooking; and there in seif 6 is explained for practical law — see there.`,
    '3#_': `(3) There. One must rinse the meat, etc. In cold countries and in a time of great cold they put water in the sun until its chill departs and afterward rinse the meat in them. Beit Yosef. Pitchei Teshuvah siman 1 letter alef. And see below seif 35.`,
    '4#_': `(4) There in hagahah. And if the butcher rinsed it, one need not rinse it in the house. And what butchers practice to wash whole ribs in mere passing to beautify them — not called rinsing. Orchot Hayim general rule 4 seif 1; Turei Chaim general rule 15 law 4; Shach s.k. 2; Peri Chadash seif 3; Beit Lechem Yehudah seif 2; Kereti seif 3; Zivchei Tzedek seif 2.`,
    '5#_': `(5) However if we know the butcher rinsed, we say presumably he rinsed well. Shach there. Kenat Hagedolah in Hagahot Beit Yosef seif 59; Peri Chadash there; Beit Lechem Yehudah there; Zivchei Tzedek seif 3 — and wrote that in Baghdad they do not have custom for the butcher to rinse at all; the main point is that in their days the butcher was the one who flays the meat, but among us the butcher does not know flaying, and therefore butchers do not rinse before flaying — what use is rinsing — see there. And in our holy city Jerusalem, although they sell flayed meat, butchers do not practice to wash the meat; rather the buyer, after he brings it home, washes and salts it.`,
    '6#_': `(6) Visible blood that fell on meat and dried and hardened — rinsing and washing alone do not suffice; rather he must rub well because of blood that dried on its surface; and one should be stringent to rub well at the time of rinsing to remove what hardened on its surface, and clean by hand, since sometimes it does not slide off the meat entirely through mere rinsing and moisture of visible blood remains on its surface even after rinsing; and so a person should teach in his house to be careful in this. Ya'avetz. And in our country in summer days, pieces of meat remain with butchers, especially neck pieces on which blood dried and does not exit with mere rinsing; rather he must remove visible blood by hand and move his hand back and forth on the meat to rub well, and be very careful in this. Zivchei Tzedek seif 4.`,
    '7#_': `(7) There. And if after he rinsed he cut, etc., he must return and rinse them. And this is when he cut with a knife, for he must return and rinse because through pressure of the knife it expels; but if he tore by hand, not required. Shach s.k. 3. Minchat Yitzchak on Turei Chaim general rule 15 seif 7; Kereti seif 4; Chochmat Adam in commentary seif 3; Pitchei Teshuvah seif 3; and the same for anything similar to a knife that expels blood through it — requires rinsing. Minchat Yitzchak there. And Peri Chadash end of seif 4 wrote that even if he did not cut with a knife but by hand, one should be concerned and must return and rinse. Beit Lechem Yehudah seif 3; Zivchei Tzedek seif 5 — and wrote: good to be careful.`,
    '8#_': `(8) If a cat ate a little of the piece, it also appears he need not rinse the place of eating — requires further study. Chochmat Adam there; Pitchei Teshuvah there. And that which he was uncertain is according to Shach's words we wrote in the previous seif that he wrote specifically with a knife; but according to Peri Chadash's words there that even cutting by hand one should be concerned — if so, all the more so with a cat's eating.`,
    '9#_': `(9) There in hagahah. Or he removed hooves of the legs, etc. — meaning shoes of an animal. Taz s.k. 3. And see above siman 68 seif 24.`,
  },
};
